content.content:
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-10",
 "criterion": "Read-only observations before an effect (registry query, GitHub release lookup, `git ls-remote`) run at most 5 attempts with exponential backoff from 2 s, capped at 300 s. HTTP 429, 5xx, rate-limited 403 and failed commands are retried, and an observation still indeterminate fails with E176 before that operation's effect.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-11",
 "criterion": "A publish command that fails with auth, rate-limit or other registry output fails the run with E163, and the error carries the last 8 lines of the command's standard output followed by its standard error. A publish run also echoes its standard output to the live log, on stderr. A failing git or gh effect command fails with E164. No effect command is re-issued automatically. Tool output shown in these errors, in the live stderr echo and in JSON errors has credentials masked as `[REDACTED]`: the values of the known credential variables, of `CARGO_REGISTRIES_*_TOKEN`, and of any variable named `*_TOKEN`, `*_SECRET` or `*_PASSWORD` whose value is at least 8 characters, URL userinfo, `npm_`, `ghp_`-family, `github_pat_` and `pypi-` tokens, `Bearer` values, and `.npmrc` `_authToken`, `_auth` and `_password` values.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-12",
 "criterion": "A `pypi` `[registries]` entry may set `publish-url`, the endpoint Twine uploads to when the index at `url` is not also the upload route. Both endpoints follow the registry URL rules of REL-PUB-05 and both enter the registry binding, so changing either makes an earlier intent stale. A package-level `publish-to` index names both routes and overrides the entry's `publish-url`. A `publish-url` on a registry that is not `pypi` is rejected when the configuration loads."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-13",
 "criterion": "A registry entry may set `publish-timeout`, the longest one publish command (`cargo publish`, `npm publish`, `twine upload`) may run, as a whole number with `s`, `m` or `h` from 1 s to 1 h; the default is 15 minutes. A command that runs longer is stopped and fails with E025. The value does not enter the registry binding, so changing it does not make an earlier intent stale. A value outside that form or range is rejected when the configuration loads.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-14",
 "criterion": "When a Cargo or npm registry publish is adopted because the exact version is already published, and the registry entry names a repository whose host, owner and name differ from the `origin` remote's (compared case-insensitively, ignoring `.git`, `git+` and URL form), Callisto prints a warning on stderr naming both repositories. The run still adopts the version. No warning is printed when the registry names no repository, when either value cannot be read as a repository, or when they match. PyPI is not checked. When the package has no tag in this repository (a first release) and the registry holds the version under a differing repository, `release` and `release --dry-run` fail with E322 before anything else, because the version is another project's; tagging the commit that set the version by hand makes the package no longer a first release."
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-15",
 "criterion": "A `[registries.<name>]` entry must set `kind` to `cargo`, `npm` or `pypi`, except the built-in keys `cratesIo`, `npm` and `pypi`, which default to their own kind. A custom registry without `kind` is rejected when the configuration loads with E321, instead of being read as an npm registry.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-16",
 "criterion": "A package this repository has never tagged that publishes to crates.io must set `description` and either `license` or `license-file` in its `Cargo.toml`, directly or from `[workspace.package]`; an empty value counts as unset. Otherwise `release` and `release --dry-run` stop with E323 naming the package and the missing field, before any publish, tag or push.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-PUB-17",
 "criterion": "Every Callisto-launched child other than Cargo has `CARGO_REGISTRIES_*_TOKEN` variables removed, matched without case sensitivity, whether inherited or supplied to a publisher subprocess. This covers ordinary, timeout, filtered, stdin, quiet and publisher routes. Cargo keeps named registry tokens for its native authentication."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-01",
 "criterion": "An operation whose result is already present and identical is recorded as already satisfied, and its effect is not repeated. For a registry version, stderr shows `warning: <package> <version> is already published; skipping`. During `release execute`, a Python release is satisfied only when every file its build produces is on the index, so a rerun after a partial upload uploads only the missing files. The check that finds a tagged package still to finish reads the version-level answer and does not build."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-02",
 "criterion": "After a run fails partway, rerunning the same command with no state file from the failed run performs only the missing operations and writes a receipt. A local annotated tag on the expected commit that is missing on the remote is pushed before the receipt is written, regardless of its annotation text. `Nothing to release.` is reported only when every operation of every package tagged on the current commit is observed already satisfied. A failure to read the local tag state other than the tag being missing stops the run with an error before any effect."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-03",
 "criterion": "Any of these fails with E167 and is never adopted: a local or remote tag on another commit, a lightweight tag, a GitHub release with a different prerelease flag, an asset of the same name with a different size or sha256, or two assets with the same name. A tag's identity is its target commit plus being annotated; annotation text is never compared.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-04",
 "criterion": "A receipt is written only when every operation ends `published` or `alreadySatisfied`, and otherwise the run exits 1 with no receipt file (E172 when operations remain incomplete). The receipt records `intentDigest`, the envelope (`orchestrationRevision`, `releaseSourceRevision`, intent and artifact-manifest digests), and each operation's outcome and provider evidence.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-05",
 "criterion": "`workflow_dispatch` with `release_source_sha` releases that commit using the current workflow run's commit as the orchestration revision. The SHA must be 40 lowercase hex characters, resolve to that same commit, lie on the triggering `main` commit's history, and equal the exact merge commit of one GitHub-verified PR merged into `main` from `callisto/version-packages` or `callisto/version-packages--<40-hex>` in the same repository. An invalid explicit source fails the candidate job without planning or updating the release PR. A valid recovery run skips `verify`, uses `recovery-checks`, and does not update the release PR before execution."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-06",
 "criterion": "A tag push that GitHub refuses because the tagged commit's workflows differ from every branch tip fails with E180, naming the tag and target commit.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-07",
 "criterion": "A local `callisto release` refuses with E320, before any effect, when the registry already holds a version it would release and the package's files differ from the commit that set that version, or no commit on the first-parent history sets it. The version is read from the package manifest or, for a Cargo package that inherits it, from the workspace manifest; files are compared by content, so a reverted change and a change made inside a merge commit count. The error names the version commit; checking it out and running `callisto release` again adopts the published version and tags that commit. A registry that cannot be read stops the release before any effect. A package whose manifest is not committed is not checked here; the clean-tree rule covers it. A package whose files match that commit, or whose version the registry does not yet hold, is unaffected.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-08",
 "criterion": "`callisto release` and `callisto release --dry-run` print a warning on stderr for each package's last tag that exists in the repository but not on `origin` (one `git ls-remote` for all of them), saying its release may not have finished. The warning does not change the plan or the exit status, and nothing is printed when there is no `origin`, no tag, or the remote cannot be read."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-09",
 "criterion": "On a main push or manual dispatch without an explicit source, the repository's release workflow builds Callisto and takes the first release from `callisto release pending` as the source; with none pending, it examines the triggering commit. It rejects a selected source that is not an ancestor of the triggering main commit or is not the exact merge commit of exactly one GitHub-verified managed PR, rather than moving on to a newer release PR. Nothing is recorded on the PR; the committed decision and provider observations remain authoritative."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-10",
 "criterion": "After `execute` succeeds, including receipt upload, the workflow dispatches the oldest unfinished release other than the one just executed, by full merge SHA; when none remains, it refreshes the managed release PR for changesets now on main. A failed execution leaves the release unfinished and selected again. A failed dispatch fails the drain job and does not refresh the release PR. The drain job receives no registry secrets."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-11",
 "criterion": "`callisto release pending` lists, oldest first, the merged release commits whose release did not finish, from the last `--limit` commits (default 10) on HEAD's first-parent line that wrote the committed decision file (`--decision`, default `.callisto/release-decision.json`). A release is finished when every package in that commit's decision that has a publish target has a tag for its version, and every such package released to GitHub has a published (not draft) release; one that cannot be read counts as unfinished. The scan stops at the newest finished release, since releases are sequential. With `--from <sha>` only that release commit and newer ones count, and a SHA that is not a release commit on the branch is an error. The command lists the commit, its packages and versions, and what is missing, in text or in JSON under `pending`; it publishes nothing and exits 0 when there are none."
}

